AB  - Total elbow arthroplasty (TEA) is a well-established surgical intervention for patients with end-stage elbow arthritis, particularly those with rheumatoid arthritis (RA). While TEA alleviates pain and improves function, there is a paucity of literature examining its comparative outcomes across different autoimmune conditions. The purpose of this study is to compare TEA perioperative outcomes and healthcare utilization between patients with RA and those with other autoimmune diseases. The Nationwide Readmissions Database (NRD) was queried from 2016 to 2022 to compare outcomes between patients undergoing TEA with RA and those with other autoimmune conditions. Patients who received a diagnosis of an autoimmune condition within 6 months of surgery were identified using International Classification of Diseases, 10th Revision Clinical Modification codes. Logistic regression was performed to identify independent predictors of postoperative complications, with significance set at P <.05 and adjusted using Bonferroni correction. Chi-square and student t -tests identified differences in categorical and continuous variables, respectively. Preoperative comorbidity analysis showed RA patients had lower rates of dementia, fluid and electrolyte disorders, and hypothyroidism, but higher rates of smoking. RA patients exhibited significantly higher 180-day complication rates (49% vs. 31%; odds ratio [OR]: 2.1 [1.4-3.1]; P <.001) and 180-day revision rates (20% vs. 6.8%; OR: 3.5 [1.7-6.8]; P <.001). Logistic regression analysis confirmed RA as an independent predictor of increased 180-day complication rates (OR: 2.7 [1.7-4.2]; P <.001) and 180-day revision rates (OR: 4.1 [2.0-8.6]; P <.001). RA patients had shorter average lengths of stay (3.0 vs. 3.8 days; P =.04) and were more likely to be discharged routinely (70% vs. 52%; P <.001). RA patients undergoing TEA demonstrated significantly higher rates of postoperative complications and revisions compared to those with other autoimmune conditions. Despite shorter hospital stays and more routine discharges, the increased complication and revision rates highlight the importance of careful risk stratification and specialized care for RA patients undergoing TEA. Orthopedic surgeons should consider these outcomes when counseling RA patients and planning their perioperative care to potentially mitigate these elevated risks.
